  "markdown_report": "At 10% progress, prioritize **matched-target stochastic prefix cropping**: a small GRU4Rec training change with an interpretable control. No validation improvement has yet been established.\n\n**Insights from the starting point.** Electronics has a 9.7% sampled wrong-rank rate, versus 1.32% for CDs_and_Vinyl. This motivates category-specific diagnosis, but training margins cannot establish validation performance. Embedding scores near 0.5 indicate mean cosine similarity near zero. They provide little justification for collapse-focused regularization. Qualitative feedback identifies stale interests and insufficient immediate relevance. Prefix removal tests historical-context dependence without assuming ownership, compatibility, or latent intent.\n\n**Insights from related work.** Recency Sampling [1] establishes precedent for recency-aware GRU4Rec training. Matched-target cropping instead changes context while holding supervised events fixed. GRU4Rec documentation [2] emphasizes recurrent-state handling. Padding must therefore contribute no recurrent updates when comparing contexts. Krichene and Rendle [3] show that sampled metrics can disagree with full-catalog rankings. Host macro validation HR@10 must remain the selection criterion, with margins serving only as diagnostics.\n\n**Framework and directions.** Organize experiments along three axes: context intervention, supervision control, and ranking evidence.\n\n| Direction | Intervention | Evidence |\n|---|---|---|\n| Context diagnosis | Equal-size prefix/suffix removal | Paired target-rank changes |\n| Context learning | Stochastic prefix cropping | Matched-target control |\n| Evidence quality | History-length stratification | Fixed host HR@10 |\n\n**Candidate ideas.** Scores are originality/potential/difficulty, each 0–10; higher difficulty means harder implementation.\n\n1. **Matched-target prefix cropping: 5/7/3.** Originality: clear context-versus-supervision distinction; familiar augmentation. Potential: inexpensive and transferable; may discard durable preferences. Difficulty: small sampler change; exact alignment needs care.\n2. **Paired context-removal probe: 3/6/2.** Originality: useful controlled comparison; standard perturbation. Potential: identifies sensitivity; does not establish causal user intent. Difficulty: few inference changes; suffix removal changes temporal gap.\n3. **Crop probability conditioned on history length: 4/6/4.** Originality: connects context length with augmentation; conventional conditioning. Potential: protects short histories; introduces another tuning choice. Difficulty: simple rule; category differences complicate calibration.\n\n**Selected experiment.** Compare cropped training against full-context training using identical targets, negatives, loss masks, batch order, and optimizer settings. For each eligible sequence, crop its oldest prefix with probability 0.5; retain at least two events. Apply the same retained-position loss mask to the control. This estimates the effect of removing older context under matched supervision; it does not isolate every effect of augmentation.\n\nKeep the corrected GRU4Rec backbone, seeds 0/42, maximum 200 epochs, and 200 batches/epoch. Deploy full histories using exactly one selected checkpoint per category; synchronize the predictor writer. Preserve all routing, diagnostic interfaces, and padding-aware readout. Select using the fixed four-category macro validation HR@10 within the shared budget.\n\nReferences: [1](https://arxiv.org/abs/2207.02643), [2](https://github.com/hidasib/GRU4Rec), [3](https://research.google/pubs/on-sampled-metrics-for-item-recommendation/).",
  "idea": {
    "description": "Train the corrected GRU4Rec with stochastic old-prefix removal, evaluated against a full-context control with identical retained-position supervision. Retain one independently trained checkpoint per category.",
    "motivation": "Qualitative feedback suggests that older interactions sometimes distract from current interests. Existing sampled margins identify difficult comparisons, especially in Electronics, but neither establish stale-context interference nor predict validation HR@10. A matched-target experiment directly tests the context hypothesis.",
    "implementation_notes": "Use the supplied baseline rather than reconstructing unavailable implementation details. Keep DATA_DIR = os.environ['GAGC_DATA_DIR'], trial-local artifacts, four-category routing, preprocessing seed 0, training seed 42, maximum epochs 200, and 200 batches per epoch. Generate crop decisions with a dedicated seeded generator so sampling does not shift baseline negative or batch RNG streams. Preserve original positive/negative alignment and normalize loss over the same selected positions in both arms. Zero cropped inputs and suppress padding-induced recurrent updates using the baseline's padding-safe GRU path. Read the final nonpadding position using position indices, never count-1. Use full histories at inference and update the embedded predictor writer whenever model behavior changes. Preserve DiagnosisProbe(view, device), run_full_diagnosis(train_loader), diagnosis_interpreter_prompt(raw_diagnosis), and all ModelView interfaces. Report only measured diagnostics and definitions; unavailable metadata remains unavailable. Experimental controls are separate trials, never fused predictors. No extra datasets, pretrained weights, hidden test access, or evaluator changes.",
    "pseudocode": "For each category and experimental arm:\n  initialize corrected GRU4Rec with seed 42\n  for each baseline batch, within 200 epochs and 200 batches/epoch:\n    obtain sequence, positive, negative from family-specific sample_batch\n    draw crop plan using a dedicated seeded generator\n    for each row:\n      identify original nonpadding positions\n      with probability 0.5, remove k oldest events\n        k is uniform from 1 through n-2 when n >= 3\n      otherwise remove none\n      construct shared loss mask over retained valid aligned positions\n    control_input = original sequence\n    crop_input = original sequence with selected prefixes zeroed\n    choose input according to experimental arm\n    features = model.log2feats(input), with padding-safe recurrence\n    compute baseline loss using unchanged positives/negatives and shared mask\n    update the single model\n  obtain fixed host validation HR@10\nCompare equal-weight four-category macro HR@10 across arms.\nDeploy one selected checkpoint per category using full-context inference.\nDiagnostic perturbations reuse the same targets and candidates; never combine scores.",
    "originality": {
      "score": 5,
      "positive": "The matched-target control distinguishes a context intervention from altered supervision in this constrained GRU4Rec setting.",
      "negative": "Prefix cropping and recency-aware training are established concepts; methodological novelty is modest."
    },
    "future_potential": {
      "score": 7,
      "positive": "An inexpensive controlled experiment can motivate more targeted context-selection mechanisms if validation supports the hypothesis.",
      "negative": "Cropping can remove persistent preferences, and qualitative feedback does not establish that stale context causes ranking errors."
    },
    "code_difficulty": {
      "score": 3,
      "positive": "The intervention primarily changes input masking and loss-mask construction while retaining the supplied architecture.",
      "negative": "Padding-safe recurrence, matched RNG streams, target alignment, and predictor consistency require careful implementation."
    }
  },
  "related_work": [
    {
      "title": "Recency Sampling for Recommendation with GRU4Rec",
      "link": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2207.02643",
      "contributions": [
        "Provides precedent for recency-aware training-example sampling in GRU4Rec."
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "Recency-based example sampling differs from removing context while preserving supervised targets.",
        "Does not establish gains for this experiment on the four pinned Amazon datasets."
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "GRU4Rec implementation and documentation",
      "link": "https://github.com/hidasib/GRU4Rec",
      "contributions": [
        "Documents recurrent recommendation implementation and state-handling considerations."
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "Session-oriented implementation assumptions require care when applied to whole-user histories.",
        "Documentation does not validate stochastic prefix cropping."
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "On Sampled Metrics for Item Recommendation",
      "link": "https://research.google/pubs/on-sampled-metrics-for-item-recommendation/",
      "contributions": [
        "Shows that sampled recommendation metrics can disagree with full-catalog model rankings.",
        "Supports separating sampled discrimination diagnostics from retrieval claims."
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "Does not justify changing the pinned host evaluator.",
        "Does not establish training margins or context sensitivity as predictors of validation improvement."
      ]
    }
  ]
